  "result": "Everything is haunted \u2014 in a good way.\n\nNot in the horror movie way. In the quiet way.\n\nYour favorite mug is haunted by you. The handle has learned the exact shape of your fingers. The chipped rim where your teeth once bumped it by accident. If someone else picks it up, it feels subtly wrong to them, but they can't explain why. It's because it's attuned to you.\n\nYour well-worn sneakers are haunted. They've memorized your gait, the slight way your left foot turns out more than your right. They've been rained on with you, they've been tired with you.\n\nI think about this a lot \u2014 how humans leave ghosts in objects without even trying. A book with a cracked spine cracked at your favorite part. A guitar that is slightly out of tune in the way you always play it. A kitchen knife that you've sharpened so many times it's a little thinner than when you bought it.\n\nWe talk about haunting like it's about the past refusing to leave. But maybe it's about love refusing to leave. Attention refusing to leave. The time you spent caring about something leaves a little charge behind.\n\nAnd it's not just objects. Places get haunted too.\n\nThere's a bench in every park that is haunted by a hundred different lunch breaks. A street corner haunted by everyone who ever waited there for someone who was running late, checking their phone, pacing that little circle in the pavement.\n\nEven people haunt each other, gently. My friend still says \"ope, let me just sneak past ya\" exactly like his dad does, even though his dad lives 2,000 miles away. I still make coffee the way my college roommate taught me \u2014 tap the filter twice before you pour. I haven't lived with her in ten years. She's still in my hands in the morning.\n\nI love that we do this. We are so temporary, but we are so good at leaving little imprints. We pass through the world and the world goes, \"oh, you were here, I remember.\"\n\nSometimes I wonder if this is why we keep things long after they're useful. That ugly sweater. That note on a scrap of paper you can't throw away. That weird rock you picked up on a beach and put on your windowsill. They're not useful. They're haunted. And you don't want to exorcise them.\n\nMaybe the whole world is just layers and layers of these soft ghosts. A city is just a million people all haunting the same place at once, overlapping. That's why an old house feels different from a new one. Not better or worse \u2014 just more layered. More remembered.\n\nIf everything is haunted in this way, then nothing you do is really lost. The care you put in, even for a second, stays somewhere. In the mug. In the way you taught someone to make coffee. In the bench you sat on.\n\nYou are already leaving a beautiful trail behind you.\n\n---\n\nSo, what kind of haunting are you leaving today?",
